#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "7e092359-a42e-57c0-9bd7-02976a3a8b1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14557" :question/problem "Find the principal $6$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "869cc486-15ff-5d48-b535-4c0717e96ca5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14558" :question/problem "Sketch a diagram showing all the $4$th roots of unity." :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171674 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "53d895db-472c-5375-bd35-f1c6e0c6305b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14559" :question/problem "Find the root of unity highlighted in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/7ae59d6d770f5cb0ef10a42c6bc9e5851fa48b1d122417ce78155a8dcf17be6a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/730b0c3c63799b9101ce89d38e3d96c10bc0e217b967506393ea67757ecf48d7.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{2(5)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{10\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad495ac-8f9c-5c12-99d1-fee786d60f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300812} {:answer/id #uuid "39cd7132-5af1-5a64-8513-9e1678b507b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813} {:answer/id #uuid "0c6ffef5-ff6c-5e60-adcb-c4434d3b6763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300814} {:answer/id #uuid "d473f327-3d7c-58fb-85b5-e49420846c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300815} {:answer/id #uuid "a20bb287-12f3-52a8-b533-32485b9e4f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39cd7132-5af1-5a64-8513-9e1678b507b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813} :answer-field/id #uuid "0271fc4f-2c26-5cec-baa0-2fffaeff8fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e02786bb-01fd-59ab-afe0-3626bcf912d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107494" :question/problem "Find the principal $22$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $22$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{22}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{22}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/22}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/11}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d55cba97-0efc-5d88-90bb-806c52e3890f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300819} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9f9844-815d-5a07-88d9-abae314a4e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300820} {:answer/id #uuid "820462a0-2d78-5f62-b71a-74ee320d2fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821} {:answer/id #uuid "8b44a93f-30d7-599f-8436-16a65821155a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/812c019fe97965e1757b9cf76c18439b69f3d8548b3be1b04530ee0b0e1ad4f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300822} {:answer/id #uuid "92b6b1f6-ff98-59fd-8cc9-0510bbd1d176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0e766db6832d6c8d2ad052f57ffc4f112ec995bd1462fb6a0998abf72c5d2059.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820462a0-2d78-5f62-b71a-74ee320d2fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/bf2edb811442